{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_l3s7cmqk7veayfunmozeflq4ghppxd75qgpcp4n4t3e44jrs475a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## Result: the translation is correct — no bugs found, no changes needed to `src/lib.rs`\n\nThe library is small (66 lines of C: a mutable file-scope `house_t` global, four `static` helpers, and two exported functions `run`/`driver`). The existing translation was already byte-accurate; my work was building the verification apparatus and proving it.\n\n### Phase A — artifacts (`translation/`)\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C exports exactly `driver` and `run`. Note `run` is *not* in `driver.h`, but it's a non-`static` definition so it has external linkage and is a genuine low-level entry point. Both are exported by Rust; **symbol diff is empty**. All six `static` C items correctly stay unexported. No module was left untranslated, no stubs added.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — 9 rows. The mechanical grep found **zero** error returns, asserts, branches, or range checks; both functions are `void`. So the error obligation inverts: the C *accepts* everything and wraps on signed overflow, and Rust must not panic where C wraps.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 22 rows across 5 axes (entry point × argument value class × accumulated global state × call-sequence shape × printed-output shape).\n\n### Phases B/C/D — 39 tests, all passing\nEvery test loads **both** `.so`s via `libloading` and compares captured `printf` bytes. The crate is `cdylib`-only, so the tests *cannot* link the Rust code directly — every call goes through `dlsym`, exercising the `#[no_mangle]` wrappers.\n\nBeyond the required matrix, I also confirmed the Rust matches the C compiled at **`-O0`, `-O2`, and `-O3`** — worth checking because `bedrooms += extra` and `floors++` are signed-overflow UB, so I wanted evidence the wrapping behavior isn't an artifact of one optimization level.\n\n### Two things worth flagging\n1. **A real harness bug I found and fixed.** My first run showed 5 \"divergences\" — all false. libtest's own progress output (`ok`, `test row3 ... `) was leaking into my `dup2` fd-1 capture from concurrent test threads; the library bytes were identical. Fixed via `.cargo/config.toml` setting `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1`, plus a harness assertion on that variable and a per-line well-formedness guard, so contamination now fails loudly instead of ever passing silently.\n\n2. **I mutation-tested the harness rather than trusting green checkmarks.** Passing tests prove nothing if the harness can't detect failure. I injected 10 deliberate bugs into the Rust (`wrapping_add`→`saturating_add`, `%.1f`→`%.2f`, wrong initializers, `bathrooms += 2.0`, dropping one of `driver`'s two `run` calls, deleting a `print_the_house`) — **all 10 were caught**, with precise byte-level diffs. I also verified the release-profile run was genuine by confirming a mutated release `.so` fails and a bogus `RUST_DRIVER_SO` path errors out.\n\nTwo design points are worth knowing if you extend this: the two libraries must be driven in **lockstep** (each step calls C then Rust with the same argument under one lock), because the global `the_house` has no reset entry point; and rows 1/22 live in their own single-test binaries since the static initializer is only observable on the first call in a fresh process.\n\n`c_src/` was not modified (source checksums unchanged; only the instructed `build/` dir added). Re-run everything with `translation/verify.sh`.\n\nOne caveat: `cargo test` alone does not build the `cdylib` that the tests `dlopen`, so `cargo build` must precede it — `verify.sh` handles this, but running `cargo test` directly on a clean tree will fail with a \"Rust cdylib not found\" assertion."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2342,"cache_read_input_tokens":104006,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2342,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"45e07446-42e1-4ca1-9deb-05b1c2a9cf48","uuid":"6a13ee41-a7c9-4818-a609-46722fa6940c","timestamp":"2026-08-28T14:16:06.156Z"}
